  "query_preview": "..\nTonight through Tuesday: Ridge pattern will continue the warm, \ndry pattern through Tuesday afternoon as a upper level trough \nwill deepen along the coast. Overnight lows will be in the upper\n40s and 50s. Tuesday highs will 80s to low 90s. Smoke from area\nwildfires will continue to bring localized vis and air quality \nimpacts mainly over the Cascades. The ridge axis slides over MT.\nThe shift will usher in breezy south to north with gusts into \nthe 20-25 mph range. A shortwave disturbance rounding the \nlongwave trough will punch through Oregon and into southeast \nWashington by Tuesday afternoon. Increasing moisture and \ndestabilizing mid levels in the atmosphere will bring a 15% \nchance for thunderstorms over the Northeast Blue Mtns into the \nPalouse, L-C Valley, Camas Prairie, and southern to central \nIdaho Panhandle. Dry low levels will mean that any thunderstorms\nwill bring strong outflow wind potential. Convective activity \nwill continue into the overnight hours on Tuesday into Wednesday\nmorning with the threat of thunderstorms expanding into \nnortheast Washington and into North Idaho. Moisture also will \nincrease with showers and thunderstorms expected to become \nprogressively wetter.\n\nWednesday through Thursday night: A more fall like weather \npattern is in store for the latter half of the workweek. The \ntrough of low pressure off of the coast continues to dig and \ncloses off with and pushes an occluded front Wednesday night \ninto Thursday through the region. Southerly winds will increase \nover the Columbia Basin and up the Okanogan Valley ahead of the \nfront for Wednesday afternoon. Winds will be a bit gusty between\nMoses Lake to Omak and Oroville with gusts up to 30-35 mph. \nDepending on timing of showers and winds, patchy blowing dust is\npossible across the Basin.\n\nSubstantial precipitation along the occluded front will be \nwidespread in areal coverage and wetting. All areas will see a \n60% chance of at least a tenth of an inch from Wednesday night \nthrough Thursday afternoon. The Cascades, northern mountains, \nextreme eastern Washington and the Idaho Panhandle will see a \ngreater than 70% chance for at least a tenth of an inch of \nrainfall over this period, and these same areas will see a \ngreater than 50% chance of at least a quarter of inch of \nprecipitation. Temperatures will cool down quite a bit as well \nwith high temperatures down into the 60s and low 70s by \nThursday. All of the moisture with cooler temperatures will \nbring the potential for fog to develop at least in the sheltered\nvalleys.\n\nFriday through Monday: Models are trending toward the closed \nlow being cut off from the Polar Jet over the weekend. Ensembles\ncontinue to retrograde off the Oregon coast. It will continue \nthe wet pattern with a push of warm air. Increased instability \nbrings potential for thunderstorm threats. Northern valley lows \ncould see areas of frost in the early morning hours. /JDC",
